L6 = {
 "id": "l06", "slug": "06-surprise-to-kl", "title": "Lesson 6 · Surprise → cross-entropy → entropy → KL divergence",
 "h1": "Surprise → cross-entropy → entropy → KL",
 "sub": "Lesson 6 · a recall warm-up, then 4 new ideas, one per chapter, each built on the last: surprise, cross-entropy loss, entropy, KL divergence. Every computation uses powers of ½, so it all works on paper. Have a pen ready.",
 "key": ["surp", "Pd", "Qd", "CE", "HP", "KL", "beta"],
 "chapters": [
  {"scene": "Recall6", "src": "l6_all", "file": "ch1-recall", "title": "Opening recall: PPO clip, GRPO groups, logistic gradient", "short": "Recall",
   "hook": {"id": "h1", "q": "From memory, three quick ones. (a) PPO: a good response (A &gt; 0) whose ratio ρ = π_θ/π_old has already reached 1.3, with ε = 0.2. What gradient does the clipped objective give it, and why? (b) GRPO: all 8 answers to a prompt get reward 1. What is each advantage, and what does this prompt add to the update? (c) Logistic regression with p = 0.8, true label y = 0, input x = 2: compute ∂L/∂w and say which way w moves.",
     "model": "(a) <b>Zero.</b> With A &gt; 0 the objective is min(ρA, clip(ρ, 0.8, 1.2)·A). Past ρ = 1.2 the clipped term wins and is constant, so the gradient is 0. That's PPO's trust region: one batch can't move a response's probability more than about 20% from the sampling policy. (b) The mean is 1, so every A = R − μ = <b>0</b> (and the std is 0). The prompt adds <b>no learning signal</b>. (c) (p − y)·x = 0.8·2 = <b>1.6</b> &gt; 0, so w ← w − η·1.6 goes down, which lowers p toward the label 0."},
   "steps": [
    ("clip", "PPO: why a ratio past 1 + ε gets no gradient", r"For a good response (\(A>0\)) the objective is \(\min(\rho A,\ \mathrm{clip}(\rho,1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)A)\). It rises with \(\rho\) until \(1+\epsilon=1.2\), then goes flat. At \(\rho=1.3\) you're on the flat part, so the gradient is <b>0</b>: the response already got its push this batch."),
    ("grpo", "GRPO: equal rewards mean zero advantage", r"GRPO's baseline is the group mean \(\mu\). If all 8 rewards are 1, then \(\mu=1\) and every \(A=R-\mu=0\). No answer beats its siblings, so the prompt gives <b>no learning signal</b>. Keep this in mind for Chapter 4."),
    ("logreg", "Logistic regression: the (p − y)x gradient", r"\(\partial L/\partial w=(p-y)\,x=(0.8-0)\cdot2=1.6\). It's positive, so \(w\leftarrow w-\eta\cdot1.6\) goes down, the score drops, and \(p\) falls toward the label 0. Lesson 7 rebuilds this formula slowly from scratch."),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c1", "q": "Same three ideas, new numbers. (a) PPO: a bad response (A &lt; 0) with ρ = 0.7 and ε = 0.2. Clipped or not, and what gradient? (b) GRPO with 4 answers and rewards [1, 0, 0, 0], using the mean as the baseline (skip the std): what is each advantage? (c) Logistic regression with p = 0.3, y = 1, x = −1: ∂L/∂w, and which way does p move after the update?",
     "model": "(a) <b>Clipped, gradient 0.</b> Take A = −1: ρA = −0.7 and clip(0.7)·A = 0.8·(−1) = −0.8. The min is −0.8, the constant clipped term, so the gradient is 0. The response has already been pushed down by more than 20%. (b) μ = 0.25, so A = [<b>0.75, −0.25, −0.25, −0.25</b>]. (c) (0.3 − 1)·(−1) = <b>0.7</b> &gt; 0, so w goes down. Because x is negative, a smaller w raises the score w·x, so p <b>rises</b> toward 1, as it should."}},
  {"scene": "Surprise", "src": "l6_all", "file": "ch2-surprise", "title": "Surprise: −log p", "short": "Surprise",
   "hook": {"id": "h2", "q": "Your wake-word model gave “Hey Guava” probability 1/2 on one clip and 1/8 on another, and both times the phrase really was said. How much more should the second clip cost the model: 4× (because 1/8 is 4× smaller than 1/2), or something else? Try to propose a cost formula.",
     "model": "Use <b>surprise = −log₂ p</b>. p = 1/2 costs 1 bit and p = 1/8 costs 3 bits, so the second costs <b>3×</b>, not 4×. Surprise counts how many halvings p sits below 1. The log is there so that independent events, whose probabilities multiply, have costs that <i>add</i>."},
   "steps": [
    ("want", "Three rules a cost should follow", r"The model gave the true outcome probability \(p\). We want a cost that is (1) <b>0</b> when \(p=1\), (2) bigger when \(p\) is smaller, and (3) <b>additive</b>: two independent clips should cost the sum of their two costs."),
    ("halvings", "Count halvings: −log₂ p", r"Count how many times you halve 1 to reach \(p\): 1 → 0, ½ → 1, ¼ → 2, ⅛ → 3. That count is $$\text{surprise}(p)=-\log_2 p$$ <b>Worked example 1:</b> \(p=\tfrac12\) costs 1 bit and \(p=\tfrac18\) costs 3 bits."),
    ("add", "Why the log: products become sums", r"For independent clips, \(P(A\text{ and }B)=P(A)\,P(B)=\tfrac12\cdot\tfrac14=\tfrac18\). <b>Worked example 2:</b> \(-\log_2\tfrac18=3=1+2\), which is the two separate surprises added up. The identity \(\log(ab)=\log a+\log b\) is exactly rule 3."),
    ("edge", "Edge case p → 0, units, and your turn", r"As \(p\to0\), surprise \(\to\infty\): being confident and wrong is punished without limit. Code uses \(\ln\) (units called nats); 1 bit = \(\ln 2\approx0.693\) nats. The base only changes the units. <details><summary>Check your last step: p = 1/16</summary>Four halvings (1 → ½ → ¼ → ⅛ → 1/16), so <b>4 bits</b> (≈ 2.77 nats).</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c2", "q": "Two independent clips. The model gave the true word probability 1/32 on the first and 1/4 on the second. (a) What is the surprise of each, in bits? (b) What is the total surprise? Compute it two ways: by adding, and from the joint probability. (c) What is the surprise of p = 1/2 in nats?",
     "model": "(a) 1/32 → <b>5 bits</b> (five halvings); 1/4 → <b>2 bits</b>. (b) 5 + 2 = <b>7 bits</b>. Joint: 1/32 · 1/4 = 1/128 = 2⁻⁷, and −log₂ of that is 7 bits ✓. (c) −ln(1/2) = ln 2 ≈ <b>0.693 nats</b>."}},
  {"scene": "CrossEnt", "src": "l6_all", "file": "ch3-cross-entropy", "title": "Cross-entropy loss, from likelihood", "short": "Cross-ent",
   "hook": {"id": "h3", "q": "A 3-way intent classifier {yes, no, repeat}. The true label is “yes”. Model A predicts [yes 1/2, no 1/4, repeat 1/4]. Model B predicts [yes 1/8, no 1/2, repeat 3/8]. Using surprise from Chapter 2, what loss would you give each model on this clip? Do the probabilities on the wrong classes matter?",
     "model": "With a one-hot label only the probability on the <b>true class</b> matters. A: −log₂(1/2) = <b>1 bit</b>. B: −log₂(1/8) = <b>3 bits</b>. That's the cross-entropy loss. The wrong classes matter only indirectly: probabilities sum to 1, so mass on wrong classes is mass taken away from the right one."},
   "steps": [
    ("likelihood", "Likelihood: how probable were the real labels?", r"Three clips; the model gave the true labels probabilities ½, ¼ and ½. If clips are independent, the probability of getting all three real labels is the product $$\tfrac12\cdot\tfrac14\cdot\tfrac12=\tfrac1{16}$$ That's the <b>likelihood</b>. Maximum likelihood means picking the model that makes it as big as possible."),
    ("log", "Take −log: likelihood becomes total surprise", r"Products of many small numbers shrink toward 0, so take \(-\log_2\): \(-\log_2\tfrac1{16}=4=1+2+1\) bits, the sum of the per-clip surprises. Divide by \(N\): $$L_{CE}=-\frac1N\sum_i\log q(y_i)=\tfrac43\approx1.33\text{ bits per clip}$$ <b>Worked example 1.</b> Maximizing likelihood is the same as minimizing this."),
    ("onehot", "One-hot labels: only the true class counts", r"<b>Worked example 2:</b> the truth is “yes”. Model A: \(-\log_2\tfrac12=1\) bit. Model B: \(-\log_2\tfrac18=3\) bits. With a one-hot \(y\), \(-\sum_k y_k\log q_k=-\log q_{\text{true}}\). <b>Edge case:</b> if \(q_{\text{true}}=0\) the loss is \(\infty\). That's why softmax never outputs an exact 0, and why label smoothing exists."),
    ("soft", "The general form H(P, Q)", r"When the truth is a distribution \(P\) (soft labels, or the data's real frequencies), weight each class's surprise by how often it is the truth: $$H(P,Q)=-\sum_k P(k)\log_2Q(k)$$ With \(P=[\tfrac12,\tfrac14,\tfrac14]\) and \(Q=[\tfrac14,\tfrac12,\tfrac14]\): \(\tfrac12\cdot2+\tfrac14\cdot1+\tfrac14\cdot2=1.75\) bits. <details><summary>Check your last step: Q = P</summary>\(\tfrac12\cdot1+\tfrac14\cdot2+\tfrac14\cdot2=\)<b>1.5 bits</b>. That's lower than 1.75: predicting the truth exactly is the cheapest. Remember this 1.5, because it comes back in Chapter 4.</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c3", "q": "Two clips. The true labels are “yes”, then “no”. The model gave “yes” probability 1/4 on clip 1 and “no” probability 1/8 on clip 2. (a) What is the average cross-entropy loss in bits? (b) The same in nats? (c) A third clip arrives where the model gave the true label probability 0.001. Roughly what happens to the average loss, and why?",
     "model": "(a) The surprises are 2 and 3 bits, so the average is <b>2.5 bits</b>. (b) 2.5 × 0.693 ≈ <b>1.73 nats</b> (the same as −(ln ¼ + ln ⅛)/2). (c) −log₂ 0.001 ≈ 10 bits, so the average jumps to (2 + 3 + 10)/3 ≈ <b>5 bits</b>. One confident mistake dominates: cross-entropy punishes overconfidence hard."}},
  {"scene": "Entropy", "src": "l6_all", "file": "ch4-entropy", "title": "Entropy: the surprise you can't avoid", "short": "Entropy",
   "hook": {"id": "h4", "q": "Two coins: a fair one, and a trick coin that always lands heads. Even if you know each coin perfectly, how surprised are you, on average, per flip? Give a number in bits for each.",
     "model": "Fair coin: each side has p = ½, which is 1 bit, so the average is <b>1 bit</b>. Trick coin: heads has p = 1, which is 0 bits every time, so <b>0 bits</b>. That average surprise under the true distribution is the <b>entropy</b> H(P)."},
   "steps": [
    ("avg", "Entropy = cross-entropy with the right model", r"Predict with the true distribution itself: $$H(P)=H(P,P)=-\sum_kP(k)\log_2P(k)$$ <b>Worked example 1:</b> a fair coin gives \(\tfrac12\cdot1+\tfrac12\cdot1=1\) bit."),
    ("three", "More outcomes", r"<b>Worked example 2:</b> \(P=[\tfrac12,\tfrac14,\tfrac14]\) gives \(\tfrac12\cdot1+\tfrac14\cdot2+\tfrac14\cdot2=1.5\) bits, the Chapter 3 answer for \(Q=P\). Uniform over 4 outcomes gives \(4\cdot\tfrac14\cdot2=2\) bits \(=\log_2 4\). <details><summary>Check your last step: P = [½, ⅛, ⅛, ¼]</summary>\(\tfrac12\cdot1+\tfrac18\cdot3+\tfrac18\cdot3+\tfrac14\cdot2=0.5+0.375+0.375+0.5=\)<b>1.75 bits</b>.</details>"),
    ("edge", "Edge case: certainty", r"\(P=[1,0,0]\): \(1\cdot\log_21=0\), and zero-probability outcomes add nothing (we count \(0\log0\) as 0, because \(x\log x\to0\)). So <b>H = 0</b>. Peaked means low entropy, spread out means high, and the maximum for \(K\) outcomes is \(\log_2K\) (uniform)."),
    ("llm", "Why you care: entropy in GRPO", r"Each next token is a distribution over the vocabulary. TRL's <code>entropy</code> metric is the average next-token entropy in nats. Your GRPO run went from 1.08 to about 0.88: the policy got more peaked. If it collapses, the 8 samples per prompt look alike, rewards tie, and advantages go to 0 (Chapter 1). Entropy is your exploration gauge."),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c4", "q": "Compute the entropy in bits of (a) P = [½, ½, 0, 0] and (b) P = [¼, ¼, ¼, ⅛, ⅛]. (c) Which one is more spread out, and what's the maximum entropy possible with 5 outcomes?",
     "model": "(a) ½·1 + ½·1 + 0 + 0 = <b>1 bit</b> (the zeros add nothing). (b) 3·(¼·2) + 2·(⅛·3) = 1.5 + 0.75 = <b>2.25 bits</b>. (c) (b) is more spread out. The maximum for 5 outcomes is log₂ 5 ≈ <b>2.32 bits</b> (uniform), and (b) is close to it."}},
  {"scene": "KLDiv", "src": "l6_all", "file": "ch5-kl-divergence", "title": "KL divergence: the gap between the two", "short": "KL",
   "hook": {"id": "h5", "q": "Same truth P = [½, ¼, ¼]. In Chapter 3, predicting with Q = [¼, ½, ¼] cost H(P,Q) = 1.75 bits per sample. In Chapter 4, predicting with P itself cost H(P) = 1.5 bits. What does the 0.25-bit gap measure? Can that gap ever be negative?",
     "model": "It's the <b>KL divergence</b>, D_KL(P‖Q) = H(P,Q) − H(P) = 0.25 bits: the extra surprise you pay per sample for using the wrong model Q instead of the truth P. It is <b>never negative</b> (no model beats the truth on average) and it is 0 only when Q = P. It's the standard answer to “how different are two distributions?”, with the caveat that it isn't symmetric."},
   "steps": [
    ("recap", "Recap: the three pieces", r"<b>Surprise</b> \(-\log q\) is the cost of one outcome. <b>Cross-entropy</b> \(H(P,Q)\) is the average surprise when the truth is \(P\) and you predict \(Q\). <b>Entropy</b> \(H(P)\) is the same average when you predict with \(P\) itself; it's the floor. For \(P=[\tfrac12,\tfrac14,\tfrac14]\) we found 1.75 and 1.5."),
    ("gap", "KL = cross-entropy − entropy", r"$$D_{KL}(P\|Q)=H(P,Q)-H(P)=\sum_kP(k)\log_2\frac{P(k)}{Q(k)}$$ <b>Worked example 1</b>, two ways: \(1.75-1.5=0.25\) bits, or term by term \(\tfrac12\log_22+\tfrac14\log_2\tfrac12+\tfrac14\log_21=0.5-0.25+0=0.25\). A single term can be negative; the sum is always ≥ 0."),
    ("asym", "Edge case: KL is not symmetric", r"<b>Worked example 2:</b> \(P=[1,0]\), \(Q=[\tfrac12,\tfrac12]\). \(D_{KL}(P\|Q)=1\cdot\log_2\tfrac{1}{1/2}=1\) bit (terms where \(P=0\) drop out). \(D_{KL}(Q\|P)=\tfrac12\log_2\tfrac{1/2}{1}+\tfrac12\log_2\tfrac{1/2}{0}=\infty\). Order matters, so it's a <i>divergence</i>, not a distance. A symmetric option is the Jensen–Shannon divergence."),
    ("train", "Why training a classifier minimizes KL", r"$$H(P,Q)=H(P)+D_{KL}(P\|Q)$$ \(H(P)\) is fixed by the data; only the model \(Q\) changes. So minimizing cross-entropy, minimizing KL to the data and maximizing likelihood are one objective with three names."),
    ("rlhf", "The KL penalty in RLHF, PPO and GRPO", r"$$\max_\theta\ \mathbb E[R]-\beta\,D_{KL}(\pi_\theta\|\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})$$ For each sampled token it's estimated by \(\log\pi_\theta(y)-\log\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y)\). \(\beta>0\) keeps the policy close to the reference model (fluent, harder to reward-hack). \(\beta=0\), as in your GRPO run, removes the leash. <details><summary>Check your last step: π_θ(y) = 1/2, π_ref(y) = 1/8</summary>\(\log_2\frac{1/2}{1/8}=\log_24=\)<b>2 bits</b>: the policy now likes this token 4× more than the reference does, and the penalty charges for that.</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c5", "q": "Truth P = [½, ½, 0, 0] and model Q = uniform [¼, ¼, ¼, ¼]. (a) Compute D_KL(P‖Q) two ways: as H(P,Q) − H(P), and term by term. (b) Compute D_KL(Q‖P). (c) In one sentence: which direction blows up if your model gives probability 0 to something that really happens?",
     "model": "(a) H(P,Q) = ½·2 + ½·2 = 2 bits and H(P) = 1 bit, so KL = <b>1 bit</b>. Term by term: ½·log₂(½ ÷ ¼) + ½·log₂(½ ÷ ¼) = ½ + ½ = 1 ✓. (b) Q puts ¼ on outcomes where P is 0, giving terms ¼·log₂(¼ ÷ 0) = ∞, so D_KL(Q‖P) = <b>∞</b>. (c) D_KL(P‖Q), with P the truth: if Q(k) = 0 where P(k) &gt; 0, the term P(k)·log(P(k)/0) is infinite, which is the same as an infinite cross-entropy loss on a confidently wrong prediction."}},
 ],
 "drill": [
  {"id": "d1", "q": "Interviewer: “How would you measure how different two distributions are, say the word distributions of two document corpora?”",
   "model": "<b>KL divergence</b>, D_KL(P‖Q) = Σ P log(P/Q): the extra bits per word you'd pay encoding corpus P with a model of Q. Mention that it's asymmetric and infinite when Q has a zero where P doesn't, so smooth the counts (add-one) or use the symmetric, bounded <b>Jensen–Shannon</b> divergence. Cosine similarity on word-count vectors is a simpler, non-probabilistic option."},
  {"id": "d2", "q": "Why do we train classifiers with cross-entropy instead of directly with KL to the data distribution?",
   "model": "They have the <b>same gradient</b>: H(P,Q) = H(P) + D_KL(P‖Q), and H(P) doesn't depend on the model. Cross-entropy is just simpler to compute (−log q of the true label)."},
  {"id": "d3", "q": "What does β control in a PPO or GRPO config, and what are the risks of β too high and of β = 0?",
   "model": "β weights the KL penalty to the reference model. <b>Too high:</b> the policy barely moves from the reference and learns little. <b>β = 0:</b> no leash, so reward can climb faster but with more risk of drift, reward hacking or degenerate text. Recipes like DAPO drop the KL term and rely on clipping instead."},
  {"id": "d4", "q": "Your model's next-token entropy drops from 1.1 to 0.2 nats during GRPO. Good or bad, and what would you check?",
   "model": "Probably <b>entropy collapse</b>: the samples for each prompt become nearly identical, rewards tie, advantages vanish and learning stalls. Check the share of groups with zero reward variance and the sample diversity. Fixes: higher sampling temperature, an entropy bonus, or a looser upper clip (DAPO's “clip-higher”)."},
 ],
 "summary": [
  "Surprise −log₂ p counts halvings below 1. Logs turn independent probabilities (×) into costs (+). p → 0 gives ∞. 1 bit = ln 2 ≈ 0.693 nats.",
  "Cross-entropy loss = average surprise at the true labels, −(1/N) Σ log q(yᵢ). Maximum likelihood = minimum cross-entropy. With one-hot labels only q(true class) counts.",
  "Entropy H(P) = average surprise when you predict with the truth itself: 0 when certain, log₂ K when uniform. In GRPO it's the exploration gauge.",
  "KL(P‖Q) = H(P,Q) − H(P) = Σ P log(P/Q): ≥ 0, not symmetric, ∞ if Q gives 0 to something P does. Classifier training minimizes it; RLHF's β·KL keeps the policy near the reference.",
 ],
}
