# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Lesson 7 (slow part 2 of the Lesson 4 split)
L7 = {
 "id": "l07", "slug": "07-mse-gd-logreg", "title": "Lesson 7 · MSE from Gaussian noise → gradient descent → logistic regression",
 "h1": "MSE → gradient descent → logistic regression",
 "sub": "Lesson 7 · a recall warm-up, then 3 new ideas, one per chapter: where squared error comes from, how gradient descent steps, and the sigmoid. The last chapter recaps the pieces and combines them into logistic regression and its (p − y)x gradient. All the numbers work on paper. Have a pen ready.",
 "key": ["yhat", "noise", "mse", "w", "eta", "grad", "z", "sig", "p"],
 "chapters": [
  {"scene": "Recall7", "src": "l7_all", "file": "ch1-recall", "title": "Opening recall: CE = H + KL, KL asymmetry, AUROC with a tie, PPO clip", "short": "Recall",
   "hook": {"id": "h1", "q": "From memory, four quick ones. (a) A model's cross-entropy on some data is 2 bits, and the data's entropy H(P) is 1.5 bits. What is D_KL(P‖Q)? Could a better model get its cross-entropy below 1.5 bits? (b) P = [½, ½] and Q = [1, 0]. Compute D_KL(Q‖P) and D_KL(P‖Q). (c) AUROC: the positives score 0.9 and 0.6, the negatives score 0.6 and 0.2. Compute it by counting pairs (careful with the tie). (d) PPO: a good response (A &gt; 0) with ratio ρ = 1.1 and ε = 0.2. Is it clipped, and does it still get a gradient?",
     "model": "(a) H(P,Q) = H(P) + D_KL, so D_KL = 2 − 1.5 = <b>0.5 bits</b>. <b>No</b>: KL ≥ 0, so H(P) = 1.5 is the floor, reached only when Q = P. (b) D_KL(Q‖P) = 1·log₂(1 ÷ ½) + 0 = <b>1 bit</b> (Q's zero term drops out). D_KL(P‖Q) = ½·log₂(½ ÷ 1) + ½·log₂(½ ÷ 0) = <b>∞</b>, because Q gives 0 to b, which P allows. (c) 2 × 2 = 4 pairs: 0.9 vs 0.6 → 1, 0.9 vs 0.2 → 1, 0.6 vs 0.6 → <b>½ (tie)</b>, 0.6 vs 0.2 → 1. AUROC = 3.5 / 4 = <b>0.875</b>. (d) <b>Not clipped.</b> 1.1 is inside [0.8, 1.2], so the objective is still ρA, which rises with ρ: the gradient is nonzero and the response keeps getting pushed up until ρ reaches 1.2."},
   "steps": [
    ("cekl", "Lesson 6: cross-entropy = entropy + KL", r"\(H(P,Q)=H(P)+D_{KL}(P\|Q)\), so \(D_{KL}=2-1.5=\)<b>0.5 bits</b>. KL is never negative, so no model gets below \(H(P)=1.5\). Training can only shrink the KL part, because \(H(P)\) is fixed by the data."),
    ("asym", "Lesson 6: KL is not symmetric", r"\(D_{KL}(Q\|P)=1\cdot\log_2\frac{1}{1/2}+0=1\) bit, since terms where the first distribution is 0 drop out. \(D_{KL}(P\|Q)\) contains \(\tfrac12\log_2\frac{1/2}{0}=\infty\). It blows up whenever the second distribution gives 0 to something the first one allows."),
    ("auroc", "Lesson 3: AUROC with a tie", r"List every positive × negative pair once: 2 × 2 = 4 pairs. A positive scoring higher counts 1, a tie counts <b>½</b>, lower counts 0. \((1+1+\tfrac12+1)/4=3.5/4=\)<b>0.875</b>. AUROC is the probability that a random positive outscores a random negative, with ties split."),
    ("clip", "Lesson 1: PPO clip, inside the range", r"For \(A>0\) the objective \(\min(\rho A,\ \mathrm{clip}(\rho,0.8,1.2)A)\) rises with \(\rho\) until 1.2 and then goes flat. \(\rho=1.1\) is still on the rising part: <b>not clipped, gradient ≠ 0</b>. Only past \(1+\epsilon\) (like Lesson 6's \(\rho=1.3\)) does the gradient become 0."),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c1", "q": "Same ideas, new numbers. (a) H(P,Q) = 3 bits and H(P) = 2 bits. What is D_KL(P‖Q), and which of the three numbers does training change? (b) AUROC with positives {0.8, 0.5, 0.3} and negatives {0.5, 0.1}: list every pair. (c) Harder PPO case: a bad response (A &lt; 0) whose ratio has risen to ρ = 1.3, with ε = 0.2. Clipped or not? (Take A = −1 and compare ρA with clip(ρ)·A.)",
     "model": "(a) D_KL = 3 − 2 = <b>1 bit</b>. Training changes H(P,Q) and D_KL by the same amount; H(P) is fixed by the data. (b) 3 × 2 = 6 pairs: 0.8 vs 0.5 → 1, 0.8 vs 0.1 → 1, 0.5 vs 0.5 → ½ (tie), 0.5 vs 0.1 → 1, 0.3 vs 0.5 → 0, 0.3 vs 0.1 → 1. AUROC = 4.5 / 6 = <b>0.75</b>. (c) <b>Not clipped.</b> ρA = −1.3 and clip(1.3)·A = 1.2·(−1) = −1.2. The min is −1.3, the unclipped term, so the gradient is nonzero and pushes the bad response's probability back down. PPO only stops moves that go <i>further</i> in the direction the advantage wants; a mistake in the wrong direction can always be undone."}},
  {"scene": "GaussMSE", "src": "l7_all", "file": "ch2-mse-gaussian", "title": "MSE from Gaussian noise", "short": "MSE",
   "hook": {"id": "h2", "q": "You fit a line that predicts how many seconds a clip takes to transcribe. Why do we train it with the <i>squared</i> error (y − ŷ)², rather than |y − ŷ| or (y − ŷ)⁴? Hint: Lesson 6 said a loss is the surprise −log p of what really happened. What would p have to look like for −log p to be a square?",
     "model": "MSE is the surprise −log p when you assume <b>truth = prediction + Gaussian (bell-curve) noise</b>. The Gaussian is p(y | ŷ) ∝ e^{−(y − ŷ)²/2σ²}, and −ln removes the e, leaving (y − ŷ)²/2σ² + a constant. Independent points multiply probabilities, so their surprises add. Drop the constants (they don't depend on the model) and what's left is the sum of squared errors. So the square comes from the bell curve. |y − ŷ| corresponds to Laplace noise, which has heavier tails and is more robust to outliers."},
   "steps": [
    ("noise", "The story: truth = prediction + noise", r"A model predicts \(\hat y\); the real \(y\) misses it by some noise, \(y-\hat y\). Small misses are common and big misses are rare. To score a model the maximum-likelihood way (Lesson 6), ask: <b>how likely was each real \(y\)</b> under this model?"),
    ("bell", "The bell curve (Gaussian)", r"$$p(y\mid\hat y)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^2}}\,e^{-\frac{(y-\hat y)^2}{2\sigma^2}}$$ Read it piece by piece: <b>e to the minus squared distance</b> gives the bell shape; \(\sigma\) is how wide the noise is; the front factor only makes the total area 1. With \(\sigma=1\), a 1σ miss is at 0.61 of the peak height and a 3σ miss at 0.01: big misses get rare fast."),
    ("neglog", "Surprise of one point: the log cancels the e", r"Loss = surprise = \(-\ln p\), and \(-\ln e^{-a}=a\), so $$-\ln p(y\mid\hat y)=\frac{(y-\hat y)^2}{2\sigma^2}+\ln\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^2}$$ <b>Worked example 1</b> (\(\sigma=1\)): a miss of 1 costs \(1^2/2=0.5\), a miss of 2 costs \(2^2/2=2\), a miss of 3 costs \(3^2/2=4.5\), each plus the same constant. Double the miss, 4× the surprise."),
    ("sum", "Many points: products become sums, which is MSE", r"Independent points: \(p(\text{all }y)=\prod_i p(y_i\mid\hat y_i)\), so the surprises add: $$-\ln p(\text{all }y)=\sum_i\frac{(y_i-\hat y_i)^2}{2\sigma^2}+N\ln\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^2}$$ \(\sigma\) and the constant don't depend on the model's predictions, so the best model minimizes \(\sum_i(y_i-\hat y_i)^2\), or equivalently $$\text{MSE}=\frac1N\sum_i(y_i-\hat y_i)^2$$ Maximum likelihood under Gaussian noise = minimum MSE."),
    ("edge", "Edge case: one outlier", r"<b>Worked example 2:</b> model A misses by 1, 1, 1, so MSE = 3/3 = <b>1</b>. Model B misses by 0, 0, 3, so MSE = 9/3 = <b>3</b>. Both have a total miss of 3, but B costs 3× more: the bell curve says a 3σ miss is nearly impossible, so one outlier dominates. With many outliers, use \(|y-\hat y|\) (MAE), which is the loss for heavier-tailed Laplace noise. <details><summary>Check your last step: misses 2, 0, −2</summary>\((4+0+4)/3=8/3\approx\)<b>2.67</b>. The sign disappears when you square.</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c2", "q": "(a) Truth y = [2, 5, 6] and predictions ŷ = [3, 5, 4]. Compute the MSE. (b) With σ = 1 and ignoring the constant, how many times bigger is the Gaussian surprise for a miss of 3 than for a miss of 1? (c) In one sentence: why does one outlier pull an MSE-trained model so hard?",
     "model": "(a) Misses: −1, 0, 2. Squares: 1, 0, 4. MSE = 5/3 ≈ <b>1.67</b>. (b) 3²/2 = 4.5 and 1²/2 = 0.5, so <b>9×</b> (the square of 3). (c) Gaussian noise says big misses are nearly impossible, so their surprise grows with the <b>square</b> of the miss: one 10-unit miss costs as much as 100 one-unit misses, and the fit bends toward it."}},
  {"scene": "GradDesc7", "src": "l7_all", "file": "ch3-gradient-descent", "title": "Gradient descent, one step at a time", "short": "Grad desc",
   "hook": {"id": "h3", "q": "The loss is L(w) = (w − 3)² and you're standing at w = 5. You can't see the curve; you can only feel the slope under your feet. (a) Which way should w move? (b) Propose a rule for how far to step. (c) What could go wrong if your steps are too big?",
     "model": "(a) <b>Toward smaller w.</b> The slope is dL/dw = 2(5 − 3) = 4 &gt; 0, so the loss rises to the right; go left. (b) <b>Gradient descent:</b> w ← w − η·dL/dw. Step against the slope, in proportion to how steep it is, scaled by the learning rate η. (c) Too large an η <b>overshoots</b> the bottom. With η = 1.1 you go 5 → 0.6 → 5.88, a little farther from 3 each time, so it diverges. Too small an η just crawls."},
   "steps": [
    ("hill", "The slope tells you which way is uphill", r"\(L(w)=(w-3)^2\) is the squared error of a one-weight model \(\hat y=w\cdot x\) on a single point \(x=1,\ y=3\) (Chapter 2). The slope is the <b>derivative</b>. Nudge test: \(L(5)=4\), \(L(5.1)=4.41\), so the slope ≈ \(0.41/0.1\approx4\). Exactly, by the chain rule \((u^2)'=2u\cdot u'\) with \(u=w-3\): $$\frac{dL}{dw}=2(w-3)=2\cdot2=4$$"),
    ("rule", "The update rule", r"$$w\leftarrow w-\eta\cdot\frac{dL}{dw}$$ A positive slope means the loss goes up to the right, so the <b>minus sign</b> steps left, downhill. \(\eta\), the <b>learning rate</b>, sets how far you step per unit of slope. A steep slope here means you're far from the bottom, so you take a bigger step."),
    ("ex1", "Worked example 1: η = 0.25", r"Start at \(w=5\). Slope 4 → \(w=5-0.25\cdot4=4\). Slope 2 → \(w=4-0.5=3.5\). Slope 1 → \(w=3.5-0.25=3.25\). The distance to the bottom halves each step: 2 → 1 → 0.5 → 0.25. Why? \(w-3\leftarrow(w-3)-\eta\cdot2(w-3)=(1-2\eta)(w-3)\), and \(1-2\cdot0.25=0.5\)."),
    ("ex2", "Worked example 2 (edge case): η too big", r"\(\eta=1.1\), start at \(w=5\). Slope 4 → \(w=5-4.4=0.6\), past the bottom. Slope \(2(0.6-3)=-4.8\) → \(w=0.6+5.28=5.88\). The distance goes 2 → 2.4 → 2.88: the multiplier \(1-2\eta=-1.2\) flips the side and grows the distance, so it <b>diverges</b>. \(\eta=1\) (multiplier −1) bounces 5, 1, 5, 1 forever. In code this looks like a loss that climbs and then turns into NaN."),
    ("faded", "Your turn, then the real thing", r"With many weights, the <b>gradient</b> \(\nabla L\) is one slope per weight and the same rule updates all of them at once: \(w\leftarrow w-\eta\nabla L\). The <code>lr = 1e-5</code> in your GRPO config is this \(\eta\). <details><summary>Check your last step: η = 0.5 from w = 5</summary>\(w=5-0.5\cdot4=\)<b>3</b>, the bottom in one step, because \(1-2\eta=0\). That's special to this perfect bowl; real losses aren't this tidy.</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c3", "q": "L(w) = (w + 1)², so dL/dw = 2(w + 1). Start at w = 1. (a) Do two steps with η = 0.25. (b) Do one step with η = 1.5. What happens? (c) The distance to the bottom gets multiplied by (1 − 2η) each step. Which learning rates converge for this loss?",
     "model": "(a) Slope 2(1 + 1) = 4 → w = 1 − 0.25·4 = <b>0</b>. Slope 2 → w = 0 − 0.5 = <b>−0.5</b>. The distance to −1 goes 2 → 1 → 0.5. (b) Slope 4 → w = 1 − 1.5·4 = <b>−5</b>: the distance goes from 2 to 4, on the other side. It overshoots and doubles, so it <b>diverges</b>. (c) It converges when |1 − 2η| &lt; 1, i.e. <b>0 &lt; η &lt; 1</b>. η = 0.5 lands in one step, η = 1 bounces forever, and above 1 it diverges."}},
  {"scene": "LogReg7", "src": "l7_all", "file": "ch4-logistic-regression", "title": "Logistic regression and the (p − y)x gradient", "short": "Log reg",
   "hook": {"id": "h4", "q": "A wake-word detector computes a score z = w·x from one feature x and must output P(wake word). (a) How would you squash any score z between −∞ and ∞ into a probability? (b) Which loss should it use for a label y ∈ {0, 1}? (Think Lesson 6.) (c) Guess the gradient dL/dw for one example.",
     "model": "(a) The <b>sigmoid</b>, p = σ(z) = 1/(1 + e^{−z}). It comes from saying that the log-odds ln(p/(1 − p)) equals the score z. (b) <b>Binary cross-entropy</b>, the surprise of the true label: −ln p if y = 1 and −ln(1 − p) if y = 0, written together as L = −[y ln p + (1 − y) ln(1 − p)]. (c) <b>dL/dw = (p − y)·x</b>: how wrong the probability is, times the input. By the chain rule, dL/dp · dp/dz · dz/dw, the sigmoid's slope p(1 − p) cancels the 1/p or 1/(1 − p) that comes from the log."},
   "steps": [
    ("recap", "Recap: the pieces, and the one that's new", r"<b>Score</b> \(z=w\cdot x\), like Chapter 3's \(\hat y=w\cdot x\). <b>Loss</b> = surprise of the true label, \(-\ln(\text{prob of true label})\) (Lesson 6). <b>Step</b> \(w\leftarrow w-\eta\,dL/dw\) (Chapter 3). The only new piece is how to turn a score into a probability."),
    ("sigmoid", "The sigmoid, from log-odds", r"Odds are \(p/(1-p)\): \(p=\tfrac34\) means odds 3:1. Let the score be the log-odds and solve for \(p\): $$\ln\frac{p}{1-p}=z\ \Rightarrow\ \frac{p}{1-p}=e^z\ \Rightarrow\ p=\frac{e^z}{1+e^z}=\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}=\sigma(z)$$ Checks: \(z=0\) → odds 1:1 → \(p=\tfrac12\). \(z=\ln3\approx1.1\) → odds 3:1 → \(p=\tfrac34\). \(z=-\ln3\) → \(p=\tfrac14\)."),
    ("loss", "The loss: binary cross-entropy", r"If \(y=1\), the loss is \(-\ln p\); if \(y=0\), it's \(-\ln(1-p)\). In one formula: $$L=-\big[y\ln p+(1-y)\ln(1-p)\big]$$ The \(y\)'s just switch the right term on. With \(p=0.8\): \(y=1\) costs \(-\ln0.8\approx0.22\) nats and \(y=0\) costs \(-\ln0.2\approx1.61\) nats."),
    ("slope", "The sigmoid's slope is p(1 − p)", r"$$\frac{dp}{dz}=\frac{e^{-z}}{(1+e^{-z})^2}=\underbrace{\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}}_{p}\cdot\underbrace{\frac{e^{-z}}{1+e^{-z}}}_{1-p}=p(1-p)$$ Check: at \(z=0\), \(p=\tfrac12\), so the slope is \(\tfrac14\), the steepest point of the S. Keep \(p(1-p)\) in mind, because it cancels in the next step."),
    ("chain", "Chain rule: why it's (p − y)x", r"$$\frac{dL}{dw}=\frac{dL}{dp}\cdot\frac{dp}{dz}\cdot\frac{dz}{dw}$$ \(y=1\): \(-\frac1p\cdot p(1-p)\cdot x=-(1-p)x=(p-1)x\). \(y=0\): \(\frac1{1-p}\cdot p(1-p)\cdot x=p\,x=(p-0)x\). Both cases are $$\frac{dL}{dw}=(p-y)\,x$$ how wrong the probability is, times the input."),
    ("ex1", "Worked example 1: one full update", r"\(x=2,\ w=0,\ y=1,\ \eta=0.5\). \(z=0\), so \(p=\sigma(0)=0.5\). Gradient \((0.5-1)\cdot2=-1\). Update \(w\leftarrow0-0.5\cdot(-1)=0.5\). Next pass: \(z=0.5\cdot2=1\), \(p=\sigma(1)\approx0.73\), up toward \(y=1\) ✓."),
    ("ex2", "Worked example 2 (edge cases)", r"With \(x=1\): <b>confident and right</b> (\(p=0.99,\ y=1\)): \((0.99-1)\cdot1=-0.01\), almost nothing to learn. <b>Confident and wrong</b> (\(p=0.99,\ y=0\)): \(0.99\), a big push, though never bigger than \(|x|\). <b>Feature absent</b> (\(x=0\)): the gradient is 0 whatever \(p\) is, so that weight doesn't move. The update is proportional to the error."),
    ("faded", "Your turn, and the same shape as MSE", r"Linear regression with \(\tfrac12(\hat y-y)^2\) has gradient \((\hat y-y)\,x\); logistic regression with cross-entropy has \((p-y)\,x\). Both are <b>(prediction − truth) × input</b>, because each loss is the right likelihood for its model. <details><summary>Check your last step: p = 0.2, y = 1, x = 3</summary>\((0.2-1)\cdot3=\)<b>−2.4</b>. The gradient is negative, so \(w\) rises by \(\eta\cdot2.4\), and \(p\) rises toward 1.</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c4", "q": "x = −1, w = 0, y = 1, η = 1. (a) Compute p, the gradient, and the new w. (b) On the next forward pass, does p go up or down? (Careful: x is negative.) (c) With x = 1, give the gradient for a confidently wrong example (p = 0.95, y = 0) and a confidently right one (p = 0.95, y = 1). Why is it good that they differ so much?",
     "model": "(a) z = 0, so p = <b>0.5</b>. Gradient (0.5 − 1)·(−1) = <b>0.5</b>. w ← 0 − 1·0.5 = <b>−0.5</b>. (b) New z = (−0.5)·(−1) = 0.5, so p = σ(0.5) ≈ 0.62: it goes <b>up</b>, toward y = 1 ✓. w went down, but because x is negative, the score went up. (c) Wrong: (0.95 − 0)·1 = <b>0.95</b>. Right: (0.95 − 1)·1 = <b>−0.05</b>. The update is proportional to the error, so learning effort goes to the examples the model gets wrong, and the ones it already gets right barely move it."}},
 ],
 "drill": [
  {"id": "d1", "q": "Coding-round warm-up: write logistic regression training in plain numpy (no sklearn). X has shape (N, d) and y has shape (N,) with 0/1 labels. Aim for about 8 lines: forward pass, loss, gradient, update.",
   "model": "<pre><code>def sigmoid(z):\n    return 1 / (1 + np.exp(-z))\n\nN, d = X.shape\nw, b = np.zeros(d), 0.0\nfor step in range(1000):\n    p = sigmoid(X @ w + b)        # (N,)\n    eps = 1e-12                   # keep log away from 0\n    loss = -np.mean(y * np.log(p + eps)\n                    + (1 - y) * np.log(1 - p + eps))\n    # the (p - y)x rule, averaged over N\n    grad_w = X.T @ (p - y) / N    # (d,)\n    grad_b = np.mean(p - y)       # bias: x = 1\n    w -= lr * grad_w\n    b -= lr * grad_b</code></pre>Say out loud: the shapes, averaging over N, the small epsilon so log never sees 0, and that the bias gradient is (p − y) with x = 1."},
  {"id": "d2", "q": "Same loop for linear regression with MSE. What changes?",
   "model": "Two lines. The forward pass has no sigmoid, <code>y_hat = X @ w + b</code>, and the loss is <code>np.mean((y_hat - y)**2)</code>. The gradient is <code>2 * X.T @ (y_hat - y) / N</code>: the same (prediction − truth) × input shape, with a 2 from the square. Using ½·MSE removes the 2."},
  {"id": "d3", "q": "Your training loss goes 0.69 → 0.65 → 3.1 → nan. What do you suspect first, and what do you try?",
   "model": "<b>The learning rate is too large</b>: the overshoot from Chapter 3 (η = 1.1), where each step lands farther away. Lower lr by 10×, and standardize the features so all the slopes are on a similar scale. Also check for <b>log(0)</b>: clip p, or compute the loss from logits (e.g. <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss</code>, which uses a stable log-sum-exp). 0.69 is ln 2, the loss of always predicting p = 0.5, a useful sanity check for the first step."},
  {"id": "d4", "q": "Why not train a classifier with MSE between σ(z) and the 0/1 label?",
   "model": "MSE assumes Gaussian noise, but a 0/1 label follows a Bernoulli distribution; cross-entropy is its likelihood. In practice: with MSE through a sigmoid the gradient is (p − y)·<b>p(1 − p)</b>·x, and when the model is confidently wrong (p ≈ 1, y = 0) the factor p(1 − p) ≈ 0, so it barely learns from its worst mistakes. Cross-entropy cancels that factor and leaves (p − y)x. The MSE version is also non-convex in w."},
 ],
 "summary": [
  "MSE = the surprise −log p under Gaussian noise (truth = prediction + bell-curve noise). The log cancels the e and leaves (y − ŷ)²/2σ² + a constant; independent points add. Squaring makes one big miss dominate; MAE corresponds to Laplace noise.",
  "Gradient descent: w ← w − η·dL/dw. Step against the slope, scaled by the learning rate. On (w − 3)² the distance to the bottom is multiplied by (1 − 2η) each step: η = 0.25 halves it, η = 1.1 diverges.",
  "Sigmoid σ(z) = 1/(1 + e^{−z}) comes from setting the log-odds ln(p/(1 − p)) equal to the score z. Its slope is p(1 − p).",
  "Logistic regression = score z = w·x → p = σ(z) → binary cross-entropy → chain rule → dL/dw = (p − y)x. Confidently wrong examples get big updates, confidently right ones almost none.",
 ],
}
LESSONS["7"] = L7

L10 = {
 "id": "l10", "slug": "10-splits-cv-leakage", "title": "Lesson 10 · Train/val/test, cross-validation and data leakage",
 "h1": "Train/val/test, cross-validation and data leakage",
 "sub": "Lesson 10 · a recall warm-up, then 3 new ideas, one per chapter: why you need three separate splits (and what goes wrong if you choose on the test set), k-fold cross-validation for small data, and data leakage, the way an honest held-out score quietly stops being honest. This is the last piece of Lesson 4, taught slowly. Have a pen ready.",
 "key": ["trn", "val", "tst", "kf", "leak", "w", "delta", "rho"],
 "chapters": [
  {"scene": "Recall10", "src": "l10_all", "file": "ch1-recall", "title": "Opening recall: backprop, PPO clip for A < 0, AUROC with a tie, overfitting", "short": "Recall",
   "hook": {"id": "h1", "q": "From memory, four quick ones. (a) Lesson 9's tiny net: z = w₁x, h = ReLU(z), ŷ = w₂h, L = ½(ŷ − y)². With x = 2, w₁ = 1, w₂ = 3, y = 4, compute ∂L/∂w₂ and ∂L/∂w₁. What happens to both if w₁ = −1? (b) PPO with ε = 0.2 and a bad response, A = −1: is ρ = 0.9 clipped? Is ρ = 0.7? (c) AUROC with positives {0.7, 0.4} and negatives {0.4, 0.3, 0.1}. (d) Train loss 0.05, validation loss 0.40. What's the diagnosis, and name two fixes.",
    "model": "(a) Forward: z = 2, h = 2, ŷ = 6, L = 2. δŷ = 6 − 4 = 2, so ∂L/∂w₂ = δŷ·h = <b>4</b>. δh = δŷ·w₂ = 6, δz = 6 (ReLU on), so ∂L/∂w₁ = δz·x = <b>12</b>. With w₁ = −1: z = −2, h = 0, so <b>both gradients are 0</b> (dead ReLU). (b) ρ = 0.9: min(−0.9, −0.9), <b>not clipped</b>, still pushed down. ρ = 0.7: min(−0.7, 0.8·(−1) = −0.8) = −0.8, the clipped term, which is flat: <b>clipped, gradient 0</b>. (c) 6 pairs: 0.7 beats all three (3), 0.4 ties 0.4 (½) and beats 0.3 and 0.1 (2), so (3 + 2.5)/6 ≈ <b>0.92</b>. (d) Low train, big gap: <b>overfitting</b> (high variance). Fixes: more data, stronger L2 or dropout, early stopping."},
   "steps": [
    ("bp", "Lesson 9: backprop on the tiny net", r"Forward and save: \(z=2,\ h=2,\ \hat y=6,\ L=\tfrac12(6-4)^2=2\). Start at the end: \(\delta_{\hat y}=\hat y-y=2\), so \(\frac{\partial L}{\partial w_2}=\delta_{\hat y}\,h=4\). Walk back: \(\delta_h=\delta_{\hat y}w_2=6\), \(\delta_z=6\cdot1=6\) (ReLU on), so \(\frac{\partial L}{\partial w_1}=\delta_z\,x=12\). Edge case: \(w_1=-1\) makes \(z=-2\) and \(h=0\), so both gradients are 0, a dead ReLU."),
    ("clip", "Lesson 1: PPO clip on the A < 0 side", r"With \(A=-1\), \(\min(\rho A,\ \mathrm{clip}(\rho,0.8,1.2)A)\). \(\rho=0.9\) is inside the range: both terms are \(-0.9\), <b>not clipped</b>, and the response is still pushed down. \(\rho=0.7\): \(\rho A=-0.7\) and \(0.8\cdot(-1)=-0.8\). The min is the clipped, flat term, so the <b>gradient is 0</b>: it has already been made rare enough for this batch."),
    ("auroc", "Lesson 3: AUROC with a tie", r"List every positive × negative pair: \(2\times3=6\). 0.7 beats 0.4, 0.3 and 0.1 → 3. 0.4 vs 0.4 is a tie → ½, and 0.4 beats 0.3 and 0.1 → 2. AUROC \(=(3+2.5)/6=5.5/6\approx\)<b>0.92</b>, the chance that a random positive outscores a random negative, with ties split."),
    ("bv", "Lesson 8: overfitting, and the bridge to today", r"Train loss low, a big gap to validation: <b>overfitting</b> (high variance). Fixes: more data, stronger L2 or dropout, early stopping. But notice that you could only <i>see</i> the gap because the validation set was data the model never trained on. Today is about keeping held-out data honest."),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c1", "q": "Same ideas, new numbers. (a) Tiny net with x = 1, w₁ = 2, w₂ = −1, y = 1: compute L, ∂L/∂w₂ and ∂L/∂w₁. (b) PPO, ε = 0.2, A = −1, ρ = 0.75: clipped or not? (c) AUROC with positives {0.8, 0.3} and negatives {0.6, 0.3}. (d) Train error 30%, validation error 31%. Would more dropout help?",
    "model": "(a) z = 2, h = 2, ŷ = −2, L = ½(−3)² = <b>4.5</b>. δŷ = −3. ∂L/∂w₂ = δŷ·h = <b>−6</b>. δh = δŷ·w₂ = 3, δz = 3 (ReLU on), ∂L/∂w₁ = 3·1 = <b>3</b>. (b) ρA = −0.75 and clip(0.75)·A = −0.8. The min is −0.8, the clipped term: <b>clipped, gradient 0</b>. (c) 4 pairs: 0.8 vs 0.6 → 1, 0.8 vs 0.3 → 1, 0.3 vs 0.6 → 0, 0.3 vs 0.3 → ½. AUROC = 2.5/4 = <b>0.625</b>. (d) Both high, tiny gap: <b>underfitting</b>. More dropout is more regularization and makes it worse; add capacity or train longer."}},
  {"scene": "Splits10", "src": "l10_all", "file": "ch2-three-splits", "title": "Train, validation, test: three jobs, three sets", "short": "Splits",
   "hook": {"id": "h2", "q": "You train three models with different regularization strengths λ, check each one on your test set, keep the best, and report its test accuracy. (a) What's wrong with that number? (b) What should you do instead? (c) If you'd tried 50 settings instead of 3, would the problem get bigger or smaller?",
    "model": "(a) You used the test set to <b>choose</b>, so the reported score is the best of 3 on that same data. Part of it is luck on those particular examples, which makes it <b>optimistic</b>. The test set has quietly become a validation set. (b) Hold out a separate <b>validation</b> set (or use cross-validation) to pick λ, then run the <b>test</b> set once at the end and report that. (c) <b>Bigger.</b> The more options you pick among, the more the winner's score reflects luck. In the chapter's example, the best of 20 coin-flip models scores about 59% on a 100-example test set."},
   "steps": [
    ("why", "Why the training score can't be trusted", r"A student who memorized last year's practice exam scores 100% on it and 55% on a new exam. A model can do the same: a big enough model can drive its <b>training</b> error to 0 by memorizing. So the training score can't tell memorizing from learning. Only data the model never saw measures <b>generalization</b>, how it does on new inputs."),
    ("jobs", "Three sets, three jobs", r"Split the data once, at the start (often 80 / 10 / 10). <b>Train</b>: the only data gradient descent sees. <b>Validation</b> (or dev): every <i>choice</i> you make, such as λ, the learning rate, the number of epochs (early stopping) or which model. <b>Test</b>: measured <b>once</b> at the very end, and that's the number you report. Validation is for deciding; test is for reporting."),
    ("ex1", "Worked example 1: choose λ on validation, report test", r"Made-up error rates. λ = 0: train 2%, val 15% (overfits). λ = 0.1: train 6%, val 9%. λ = 1: train 14%, val 16% (underfits). Lowest validation error → choose <b>λ = 0.1</b>. Then run the test set once: 10%. Report <b>10%</b>, not 9%. The 9% is a little flattering because you picked the best of three on the validation set, which is exactly why the test set has to be separate."),
    ("ex2", "Worked example 2 (edge case): choosing on the test set", r"20 useless models that guess at random, each scored on the same 100 test examples. Each one's accuracy is about 50%, give or take \(\sqrt{0.5\cdot0.5/100}=0.05\), so 5 points. By luck alone, the best of the 20 lands near <b>59%</b>, almost 2 standard deviations up. On fresh data it's 50%. If you pick the winner <i>on the test set</i>, its test score is no longer an honest estimate. Every look at the test set that changes a decision leaks a little of it into your choices."),
    ("faded", "Your turn: which number do you report?", r"Made-up numbers: model A has val 88% and test 85%; model B has val 90% and test 83%. Step 1, choose by validation: <b>model B</b>. <details><summary>Check your last step: what do you report?</summary>Report <b>model B's test score, 83%</b>. Switching to A after seeing that its test score is higher means choosing on the test set, the same mistake as example 2. If you really doubt B, collect a new test set.</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c2", "q": "A teammate tries 5 learning rates for a GRPO run, checks each one's accuracy on the GSM8K test set, and reports the best: 71%. (a) What's the problem? (b) How would you redo it? (c) Your lab has run hundreds of experiments over a year, always looking at the same test set. What happens to that test set, and what can you do?",
    "model": "(a) The test set was used to <b>choose</b> the learning rate, so 71% is the best of 5 on that data: <b>optimistically biased</b>, and not a clean estimate of new-question accuracy. (b) Hold out a <b>dev split</b> (for example, some of the training questions) to pick the learning rate, then evaluate the chosen run on the test set <b>once</b> and report that. (c) The test set slowly <b>turns into a validation set</b>: hundreds of decisions each leaked a little. Keep a fresh, rarely touched test set (or a private holdout) for final claims, and treat old benchmark numbers with some suspicion."}},
  {"scene": "KFold10", "src": "l10_all", "file": "ch3-k-fold-cv", "title": "k-fold cross-validation", "short": "k-fold CV",
   "hook": {"id": "h3", "q": "You only have 50 labelled clips. If you hold out 10 for validation and a model gets 8 of them right (80%), how much would you trust that 80%? Can you think of a way to use all 50 clips for validation, without ever grading a model on clips it trained on?",
    "model": "Not much. With 10 clips, <b>one clip moves the score by 10 points</b> (70% or 90%), and only 40 clips are left for training. The fix is <b>k-fold cross-validation</b>: split the 50 into k = 5 folds of 10. Train 5 times, each time on 4 folds, and validate on the fold left out. Every clip is validated exactly once by a model that didn't train on it. Average the 5 scores, and look at their spread."},
   "steps": [
    ("why", "Why one small validation split isn't enough", r"With 50 clips, a 10-clip validation set is a noisy measurement: 8 of 10 is 80%, and one clip flips it to 70% or 90%. Meanwhile a fifth of your scarce data never gets trained on. We want a validation score that uses every example, yet never grades a model on data it trained on."),
    ("how", "How k-fold works", r"Split the training data into \(k\) equal <b>folds</b> (here \(k=5\)). Run \(k\) trainings: in run \(i\), train on the other \(k-1\) folds and validate on fold \(i\). Every example is validated exactly once, by a model that never saw it. The CV score is the <b>average</b> of the \(k\) validation scores. The test set stays out of all of this."),
    ("ex1", "Worked example 1: average the folds", r"100 examples, 5 folds of 20. The five validation accuracies are 80, 85, 75, 90, 80. $$\text{CV score}=\frac{80+85+75+90+80}{5}=\frac{410}{5}=82\%$$ The spread, 75% to 90%, is useful too: that's how much a single 20-example split could have misled you. When comparing two settings, a difference smaller than this spread is mostly noise."),
    ("ex2", "Worked example 2 (edge case): rare positives", r"100 clips with only 10 positives, 5 folds. A random split can deal out 4, 3, 3, 0 and 0 positives, and a fold with no positives gives recall \(=0/0\), undefined. <b>Stratified k-fold</b> deals each class out evenly: \(10\div5=2\) positives and \(90\div5=18\) negatives per fold, so every fold keeps the 10% positive rate."),
    ("cost", "What it costs, and what comes after", r"k-fold costs <b>k trainings per setting</b>. Trying 4 values of λ with 5 folds is \(4\times5=20\) trainings, plus 1 final retrain of the winner on <b>all</b> the training data, so 21. Then the untouched test set, once. CV replaces the validation split, never the test set. \(k=N\) is leave-one-out. For big models (LLM fine-tuning) one run is already expensive, so people use a single fixed dev set instead."),
    ("faded", "Your turn: a 4-fold average", r"Four folds score 70, 74, 78 and 74. <details><summary>Check your last step: the CV score</summary>\((70+74+78+74)/4=296/4=\)<b>74%</b>, with a spread of 70–78%.</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c3", "q": "(a) 5-fold scores are 60, 70, 65, 75, 80. Give the CV score and the spread. A second setting scores 71% on average. Is it clearly better? (b) You try 3 learning rates with 5-fold CV and then retrain the winner. How many trainings is that in total? (c) 50 examples, 5 of them positive, 5 folds. What does stratification give each fold, and why does it matter?",
    "model": "(a) (60 + 70 + 65 + 75 + 80)/5 = 350/5 = <b>70%</b>, spread 60–80%. 71% vs 70% is <b>well inside the spread</b>, so it's not clearly better; it's noise. (b) 3 × 5 = 15, plus 1 final retrain = <b>16</b>. (c) <b>1 positive and 9 negatives per fold</b>. A random split could leave a fold with 0 positives, where recall is 0/0, and the fold scores would swing wildly."}},
  {"scene": "Leak10", "src": "l10_all", "file": "ch4-data-leakage", "title": "Data leakage: when test information sneaks into training", "short": "Leakage",
   "hook": {"id": "h4", "q": "You train a model to detect a frustrated caller from 5,000 call clips recorded from 200 callers. A random 80/20 split gives 95% accuracy, but in production it's 70%. Give two different reasons the held-out score could have been too good.",
    "model": "Some good answers. (1) <b>Group leakage</b>: the random split put clips from the same callers in both train and test, so the model learned to recognize those voices rather than frustration. Split <b>by caller</b>. (2) <b>Preprocessing leakage</b>: normalization statistics, a vocabulary or a feature selection were computed on all the data, test included. Fit them on train only. (3) <b>Target leakage</b>: a feature that only exists because of the label, such as whether the call was escalated. (4) <b>Time</b>: the production calls come later, and the test set didn't. Split by date. (A distribution shift in production is also possible, but that isn't leakage.)"},
   "steps": [
    ("def", "What leakage is", r"<b>Leakage</b> is any information the model couldn't have at prediction time that reaches training: from the validation or test data, from the future, or from the label itself. The held-out score looks great, then collapses in production. The question to ask about every feature and every preprocessing step: <i>could the model know this at the moment it predicts?</i>"),
    ("ex1", "Worked example 1: a scaler fit on everything", r"Standardizing a feature means \(z=(x-\mu)/\sigma\). Train values 2, 4, 6; test value 12. <b>Right:</b> use train only. \(\mu=(2+4+6)/3=4\) and \(\sigma=\sqrt{(4+0+4)/3}=\sqrt{8/3}\approx1.63\), so the test point gets \(z=(12-4)/1.63\approx4.9\). <b>Leaky:</b> \(\mu=(2+4+6+12)/4=6\). The test point moved the statistics the model trains with. The rule: fit every preprocessing step (scaling, vocabulary, imputation, feature selection) on the training data, then apply it to val and test. In CV, refit it inside each fold, which is what an sklearn <code>Pipeline</code> does for you."),
    ("ex2", "Worked example 2 (edge case): group leakage", r"Made-up numbers. 5,000 clips from 50 speakers. A random split puts clips from speaker A in both train and test, so the model can partly memorize each voice: WER 6%. Split <b>by speaker</b>, so the test set has only new voices, and WER is 15%, which is what new callers will actually get. The same goes for patients, users, documents and near-duplicate prompts: if examples come in groups, split by group (<code>GroupKFold</code>)."),
    ("kinds", "Three more kinds to recognize", r"<b>Time:</b> shuffling a time series lets the model peek at the future, so train on earlier dates and test on later ones. <b>Target leakage:</b> a feature that exists because of the label, such as <code>refund_issued</code> when predicting fraud; drop it. <b>LLM contamination:</b> benchmark questions (say GSM8K) that ended up in the pre-training or fine-tuning data. Deduplicate by n-gram overlap or near-duplicate search, and split your GRPO prompts into train and eval <i>before</i> any augmentation."),
    ("faded", "Your turn: spot the leak", r"1,000 features of pure random noise, 100 examples. Step 1: keep the 20 features most correlated with the label, computed on all 100 examples. Step 2: 5-fold CV gives 85% accuracy, on noise. <details><summary>Check your last step: where's the leak?</summary>Step 1 <b>saw the labels of the validation folds</b>. Among 1,000 noise features, some correlate with the label by chance, and selection picked exactly those, using the very examples CV later grades. Do the selection <b>inside each fold</b>, on that fold's training part only, and the CV score drops to about 50%.</details>"),
   ],
   "check": {"id": "c4", "q": "(a) Train values 1, 3, 5 and a test value of 9. Standardize the test value the right way. What mean would the leaky way use? (b) For each case, name the kind of leak and the fix: (i) X-rays from the same patient end up in train and test; (ii) you predict next week's sales with a randomly shuffled split; (iii) a churn model uses the feature 'support calls after cancelling'. (c) For your own GRPO runs: how would you make sure the eval prompts don't leak into training?",
    "model": "(a) μ = 3 and σ = √((4 + 0 + 4)/3) ≈ 1.63, so z = (9 − 3)/1.63 ≈ <b>3.7</b>. The leaky way uses μ = (1 + 3 + 5 + 9)/4 = <b>4.5</b>. (b) (i) <b>Group leakage</b>: split by patient. (ii) <b>Time leakage</b>: train on earlier weeks, test on later ones. (iii) <b>Target leakage</b>: that feature only exists after the outcome, so drop it. (c) Split the prompts into train and eval <b>before</b> anything else (augmentation, paraphrasing), deduplicate near-duplicates across the split (n-gram or embedding similarity), check that public benchmarks you report aren't in the training set, and choose hyperparameters on a dev split, not on the final eval."}},
 ],
 "drill": [
  {"id": "d1", "q": "Coding-round warm-up: write k-fold cross-validation in plain numpy (no sklearn). You're given X (N, d), y (N,), and functions fit(X, y) → model and score(model, X, y) → float. Return the mean and std of the k fold scores.",
   "model": "<pre><code>def kfold_cv(X, y, fit, score, k=5, seed=0):\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    idx = rng.permutation(len(X))       # shuffle once\n    folds = np.array_split(idx, k)      # k nearly equal folds\n    scores = []\n    for i in range(k):\n        val = folds[i]\n        tr = np.concatenate([folds[j] for j in range(k) if j != i])\n        model = fit(X[tr], y[tr])       # fit (and preprocess) on train part only\n        scores.append(score(model, X[val], y[val]))\n    return np.mean(scores), np.std(scores)</code></pre>Say out loud: shuffle first (unless it's time-ordered data), <code>array_split</code> handles N not divisible by k, any scaler is fit inside <code>fit</code> on the training part, and the test set never enters this function."},
  {"id": "d2", "q": "You standardize features with a StandardScaler. Where exactly does it go when you run 5-fold cross-validation, and why?",
   "model": "<b>Inside each fold</b>: fit the scaler on that fold's training part, then transform both the training part and the held-out fold. Fitting it once on all the data leaks the held-out fold's mean and std into training (Chapter 4, example 1). In sklearn, put it in a <code>Pipeline([('scale', StandardScaler()), ('clf', LogisticRegression())])</code> and pass the pipeline to <code>cross_val_score</code>; the pipeline refits the scaler in every fold."},
  {"id": "d3", "q": "When is a plain random split the wrong choice? Name three cases and the split you'd use instead.",
   "model": "(1) <b>Grouped data</b> (several clips per speaker, scans per patient, messages per user): split by group (<code>GroupKFold</code>). (2) <b>Time series or anything you'll deploy forward in time</b>: split by time, train on the past and test on the future (<code>TimeSeriesSplit</code>). (3) <b>Rare classes</b>: stratify so each fold keeps the class ratio (<code>StratifiedKFold</code>). Bonus: <b>duplicates or near-duplicates</b> (common in scraped text and LLM prompts): deduplicate before splitting."},
  {"id": "d4", "q": "Your first model gets 99% validation accuracy. What do you check before celebrating?",
   "model": "(1) The <b>accuracy trap</b> (Lesson 3): what's the class balance? If 99% of examples are negative, always predicting negative gets 99%. Look at precision, recall and AUROC. (2) <b>Leakage</b>: duplicates across splits, same-group examples in train and val, preprocessing fit on all the data, and features that encode the label (sort features by importance and ask whether each could be known at prediction time). (3) The <b>split itself</b>: was it random when it should have been by time or by group? (4) A sanity baseline: a trivial model, or a model trained on shuffled labels, which should drop to chance."},
  {"id": "d5", "q": "Post-training angle: how would you build an evaluation set for a GRPO fine-tune so that the score you report is honest?",
   "model": "Split the prompt pool into <b>train / dev / test before</b> generating any augmentations or paraphrases, ideally by source or template (a group split) so near-identical prompts don't straddle the split. <b>Deduplicate</b> across splits with n-gram or embedding similarity, and check public benchmarks for contamination against the training data. Tune the learning rate, β and the number of steps on <b>dev</b>, then run <b>test once</b> and report it, ideally with a confidence interval, because a few hundred prompts give a noisy score (Chapter 2's ±5 points on 100 examples)."},
 ],
 "summary": [
  "Three splits, three jobs: train fits the weights, validation makes every choice (λ, learning rate, epochs, which model), and test is run once at the end and reported. The training score can't tell memorizing from learning.",
  "Choosing on the test set makes its score optimistic, because the winner partly won by luck. The best of 20 coin-flip models scores about 59% on 100 examples, and the more options you try, the bigger the bias.",
  "k-fold CV: k folds, k trainings, each validated on the fold it didn't see; report the mean and look at the spread. Stratify for rare classes. Cost is k trainings per setting plus one final retrain, and the test set stays untouched.",
  "Leakage = information the model couldn't have at prediction time: preprocessing fit on all the data, group overlap (same speaker in train and test), shuffled time series, label-derived features, and benchmark contamination. Fit preprocessing inside each fold, and split by group or time when the data calls for it.",
 ],
}
